  "headline": "The ~11.6% apparent-activation-energy deficit in the signed Phase-0 pot-life Arrhenius KPI is not two-channel cure chemistry, and it is not a uniform rescaling of Ea: it is curvature. ln(pot life) vs 1/T is convex in 12/12 locked systems (mean c2 = 3.78e+06), an effect 22.4 sigma beyond a hostile null in which the truth is exactly Arrhenius and the only departure is reported-precision rounding (0/20000 draws). The Kamal-Sourour (1973) autocatalytic two-channel law, enumerated over 25080 cells spanning threshold conversions from 0.05 to the structural Flory-Stockmayer gel conversion, reproduces the deficit in 2200 cells but the observed curvature in NONE: its largest positive curvature anywhere on the grid is 1.72e+05, 22x too small. Kamal-Sourour is REFUTED as the mechanism. A Vogel-Fulcher-Tammann form with a single shared T0 = 203.5 K reproduces both the curvature sign (12/12) and the deficit (rho 0.8841 model vs 0.8841 observed) in sample, but is NOT exploitable out of sample: T0 is unidentified from three training temperatures and VFT is the worst of three models on the 40C extrapolating fold. Operationally the incumbent is near-unbiased below the 73 degF anchor (-2.6% at 10C) and one-sidedly conservative above it (-15.6% at 30C, -25.1% at 40C, under-predicting pot life in 12/12 systems at both), which is the fail-safe direction for a pot-life constraint; but the 40C leg's MAPE 0.2513 breaches the module's own 0.25 held-out-temperature ceiling, a ceiling the signed KPI evaluated on the 30C leg only (0.1563).",
  "module": "amphora-cure-potlife-gate",
  "non_circularity": "Activation energies are external published constants read from the locked known-answer file; they are never fitted. The curvature estimator is applied identically to the locked data and to every model's forward prediction. The Kamal-Sourour grid is enumerated over a pre-declared physical envelope, not tuned to the data. Flory-Stockmayer gel conversions come from amine-hydrogen functionality (molecular structure), not from any measurement.",
